{"answer":"Exactly 7 subsets have sum 9. One example is {1,8}.\n\nThe full list is {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}. This is exhaustive because a subset with four or more distinct positive elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10; the possible two- and three-element subsets are exhaustively enumerated below.","approaches":[{"description":"Enumerate subsets by their cardinality, using the minimum possible sum to exclude cardinality at least four.","id":"cardinality-enumeration","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It produces all possible subset sizes and enumerates each remaining case."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by steps exclude-large, pairs, triples, and combine.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"count-seven","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"There are exactly 7 subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose elements sum to 9.","step_ids":["exclude-large","pairs","triples","combine"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Enumerated in steps pairs and triples.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["count-seven"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"complete-list","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The seven subsets are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["pairs","triples","combine"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"exclude-large","justification":"The empty set has sum 0 and a singleton from the given set has sum at most 8. A subset with at least four elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Thus only two- and three-element subsets can have sum 9.","statement":"Reduce the enumeration to subsets of cardinality two or three."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["exclude-large"],"id":"pairs","justification":"For a two-element subset {a,b} with a<b and a+b=9, the possible smaller elements are a=1,2,3,4, yielding respectively b=8,7,6,5. No a>=5 can satisfy a<b and a+b=9.","statement":"The two-element subsets are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["exclude-large"],"id":"triples","justification":"Write a<b<c and a+b+c=9. If a=1, then b=2 gives c=6 and b=3 gives c=5; b>=4 would give c<=4, violating b<c. If a=2, then b=3 gives c=4; b>=4 gives c<=3, again violating b<c. If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12. Hence these are all possibilities.","statement":"The three-element subsets are exactly {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["exclude-large","pairs","triples"],"id":"combine","justification":"The exhaustive cardinality reduction leaves only the four pairs and three triples listed in the preceding steps, and all seven have sum 9.","statement":"Combining the disjoint two- and three-element cases gives 4+3=7 subsets."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
